# EFI environment: run the payload the ABL wrote into a firmware partition.
#
# The chain this exercises, in order:
#   fastboot flash logfs hello.efi   -> ABL resolves "logfs" and writes it raw
#   scsi read ... 0x19391            -> U-Boot reads that partition by LBA
#   bootefi 0xa0000000:400000        -> EFI_LOADER relocates the PE32+ image
#
# logfs is 8 MiB at LUN4 LBA 0x19391 -- measured with `part list scsi 4`, not
# guessed. It holds a kernel log on a stock handset and nothing at all in this
# project, which makes it the disposable landing pad for payload tests. boot
# (0xc505) is left alone: it is the partition the ABL boots us from.
#
# bootefi needs the image size spelled out. Without a ":<size>" suffix it only
# accepts an address the EFI loader itself put there and answers "No UEFI binary
# known at ...", which is what the first attempt hit. The size is an upper
# bound for PE parsing, not the file length, so one generous constant covers
# every payload up to 4 MiB: 0x400 blocks x 4 KiB = 4 MiB read, 0x400000 bytes
# offered to the loader. scsi read parses its numbers as hex, like every other
# block command.
bootdelay=1
bootretry=-1

stdin=serial,button-kbd
stdout=serial,vidconsole
stderr=serial,vidconsole

preboot=echo; echo RMX1971 efi

bootcmd=echo === logfs payload;scsi dev 4;scsi read 0xa0000000 0x19391 400;md.b 0xa0000000 16;echo === bootefi;bootefi 0xa0000000:400000;echo === after bootefi;sleep 40;reset
